items.checks:
The guard calls the provider's Naming hook on a probe model, with names left as the templates spell them. It prints the client with 0 and 1 options and reads the constructor call in the forms' own notation. Then it checks:
- the declaration the call runs exists (calls.ctor maps rb `new` to `initialize`; a class called as itself runs ctorDecl);
- it is not on an instance;
- it is called through the separator `calls.on[decl.on]` gives;
- its qualifier is bound by the imports the provider prints (imports printer, else go's program printer) or is the client type or module.
The same holds for the reference's {ctor} lines, the test constructors, ctorAlts, and for the accessor and method in every operation call the call printer prints. With no options the printer may use a declared ctorAlt (ocaml make0). The report says whether layout.ctor goes through tm or is a hook literal or generator function (lua sdk.new, clojure api/make-sdk, ocaml Sdk_client.make).

In the shared framework, for every language:
- an unwrap in facts.record must be facts.unwrap verbatim, as a whole word;
- every other member or type it names (zig asEntity, c vt) must be declared somewhere in the runtime, by a generic declaration search, or be cited in recordParts;
- a record of more than one part must be printed whole by the expr printer;
- the print printer must unwrap with facts.unwrap;
- facts.unwrap's quote must hold it verbatim, operators included.

NEW_FACTS in account.ts holds the keys the guard expects:
- accessor.cached: cited, may come ahead;
- streamSignal: cited, may come ahead;
- memberNamed: derived (bare iff calls.args is space or lisp);
- (client|entity)[i].field: derived from the runtime declaration;
- deleted: derived (a member or field the entity declares);
- direct.*: cited;
- imports.*: cited.
Any ref.facts value instance/type/module (the heading) is derived from the client members' `on`; entityBy is excluded. copy may be cited ahead only on a match getter.

